# Differentiating Between Submit Buttons in PHP Forms

You are facing a classic form handling scenario: you need to capture user input, but the action taken depends entirely on which button triggered the request. In your specific case, the buttons are images rather than standard `<input>` elements with visible text. This visual constraint prevents you from using the `value` attribute to carry data, as doing so would break the image rendering. Fortunately, modern web development offers a robust solution that relies on form submission conventions rather than relying on hidden values or complex workarounds.

The core mechanism here is simple: every HTTP POST request contains an input stream where the browser serializes all submitted controls into a string format. Even if a button's `value` attribute is empty, the browser still sends the name of the control. To leverage this, you must assign a unique `name` attribute to each submit button.

In your current HTML structure, both buttons likely lack explicit `name` attributes or share the same one implicitly (often defaulting to "submit"). If you inspect the network traffic or use a tool like `var_dump($_POST)`, you will see that without distinct names, the server receives a single entry indicating a submission occurred, but it cannot distinguish the intent.

To resolve this while keeping the buttons as images, update your HTML to include unique names. You can do this by wrapping the image in a label or using a hidden input that matches the button's ID, but the cleanest approach is often to treat the image element itself as having a name attribute if supported, or simply ensure the `<input type="submit">` elements surrounding the images (if any) carry the logic. However, since you mentioned the buttons are images, the most direct fix for an `<img>` tag acting as a button is to use JavaScript to handle the click event and submit the form with specific data.

If you prefer sticking strictly to server-side parsing without JavaScript, consider this pattern:

```html
<!-- Button 1: Save Draft -->
<input type="submit" name="action" value="draft">
<!-- Replace this input with your image for visual purposes if needed, 
     but ensure the name="action" is present in the submission payload. -->

<!-- Button 2: Publish -->
<input type="submit" name="action" value="publish">
```

Since you cannot place text directly on the image, a common and reliable pattern is to use JavaScript to attach a `click` listener to the images. When the user clicks the "Save" image, the script submits the form with `_action=draft`. When they click "Publish", it submits with `_action=publish`.

```javascript
document.getElementById('save-btn').addEventListener('click', function() {
    this.form.action = '/your-form-url';
    this.form.submit();
});

document.getElementById('publish-btn').addEventListener('click', function() {
    var formData = new FormData(this.form);
    formData.append('_action', 'publish');

    // Or simply redirect to the form URL with a query param if your route supports it
    window.location.href = this.form.action + '?_action=publish';
});
```

If you want to avoid JavaScript entirely, you can wrap each image in an invisible `<input type="hidden">` that shares the same name as the submit action. However, the most standard approach when images are used as buttons is to rely on the `name` attribute of a hidden input that gets populated via JS, or simply accept that the form will always be submitted and check for the presence of an `_action` field in your PHP logic.

In your PHP handler, you then check this value:

```php
if (isset($_POST['action'])) {
    if ($_POST['action'] === 'draft') {
        // Logic to save without publishing
        $status = 'draft';
    } elseif ($_POST['action'] === 'publish') {
        // Logic to publish
        $status = 'published';
    }
} else {
    // Handle standard submit or default case
    $status = 'default';
}
```

This approach is cleaner than trying to parse the request method or relying on the order of inputs. It explicitly communicates user intent through the form payload. If you are using Laravel, you might utilize a `Route::post` closure that checks `$request->input('action')`. This pattern scales well if you later add more actions like "delete" or "edit".

The trade-off here is the slight increase in complexity on the client side to ensure the correct parameter is sent. However, it avoids the pitfalls of relying on button order or hidden fields that might be stripped by certain form builders. By giving each submission path a distinct identifier, you maintain separation of concerns between the UI layer and the business logic in your database operations.
